The period from 1538 to 1606 covers the years between the [[Treaty of Anping]] and the collapse of the gold revenue. In it Hachinawa ceased to be a regional empire sustained by tribute and became a state in debt to the foreign commerce that had enriched it.

The period divides into three phases. In the first, the empire completed the subjection of its neighbours with the means it had bought in the preceding eighty years. In the second, the court attempted to remake itself on the Zong model, adopting the Zong script, the Zong manner and a new royal assembly, and enacting a constitutional framework that fixed the succession and set out the law, courts and legislation of the state. The reorganisation provoked the opposition of the lineages and priests whose authority rested on the institutions being displaced. In the third, the revenue that had paid for all of it failed, and the state turned to selling its own frontier land to the merchants of the power it had spent a century learning from.

== After the treaty ==

The [[Treaty of Anping]] of 1538 recognised the Hachina king as a sovereign and the Zong as holding a fixed coastal presence. Its immediate effect was to make the empire the principal ally of the Zong colony, and the two states campaigned together in the [[First Mahik War]] that followed.

[[Nachiha]], son of the king and later king himself, led the embassy to [[Anping]] in 1538. He travelled to the Zong colony, and the visit is the beginning of the Hachina court's direct acquaintance with Zong institutions. The mission was diplomatic, but what impressed the court was administrative: the offices, the registers, the assembly, and the fact that a state could be governed by writing rather than by proclamation and memory.

[[Tsulhachi]] died in 1541, having reigned since 1457. His son succeeded him, and the succession was the last in which the ''nawe'' exercised a real choice. In the following decades the crown's dependence on foreign revenue and foreign instruction made the election an obstacle to policy, and the practice narrowed toward the hereditary.

The change was made by legislation. The court of [[Nachiha]] produced a constitutional framework in this period which fixed the rules of succession among other things, and it is in that framework rather than in any single decree that the elective principle was displaced. The Hiknawe was the crown's answer to what the loss of the election left behind, and is treated below.

== Subjection of the tributaries ==

The firearms and horses acquired in the preceding period allowed Hachinawa to complete what it had been attempting for centuries.

The Uwisa states were reduced. The two rival river-states, [[Hatso]] and [[Kalihą]], had been played against one another for generations; the empire now possessed means of coercion that the rivalry could not absorb, and the gold assessments were raised and enforced. The [[Yelani]] of the mountains entered a tributary relationship whose terms were fixed by treaty rather than by campaign.

By the middle of the century the empire's frontiers were settled and its tributaries paying. It was the high point of Hachina power over its neighbours, and it rested on a revenue that had begun to fail.

== Zongification ==

The court's adoption of Zong ways in this period was a policy of the king, not a gradual influence, and it was understood as such by those it displaced.

=== Script decree ===

The most consequential act was the displacement of the traditional script. The Hachina had possessed a logographic script of their own for eight centuries, with a calendar and a system of numerals, and had maintained institutions for teaching it since its invention.

By royal decree the Zong script was adopted for the writing of the Hachina language, and the traditional script was displaced from official use. The reasoning was competitive: a state that could read the Zong papers, correspond with the colonial administration in its own characters, and hold its own records in the same form as its creditors was a state that dealt with the Zong on better terms. The decree was the king's, and it was imposed rather than agreed.

The decree is the origin of the period's political division. The traditional script was taught in the temple schools and kept by the priestly lineages, and the calendar it served was performed by the king. Displacing it transferred to the crown and its scribes a competence that had belonged to the temples.

=== Court and coast ===

Zong manners followed the script. The court adopted Zong dress, Zong forms of address, Zong furnishings and Zong ceremony, and the coast, where the trade was, followed the court.

The result was a society divided less by class than by position. The court, the officials of the capital and the mercantile houses of the coast became increasingly Zong in their habits; the interior, the temple establishments and the lineages of the plain did not. A man of the plain might live in the same kingdom as a man of the capital and share with him neither dress nor script nor manner.

=== Constitutional framework ===

The court of [[Nachiha]] produced in this period a body of law that is the first recorded constitutional instrument in the history of Hachinawa, and the first attempt by the state to set out its own institutions in writing rather than to govern by the practice of the court.

The framework established four things:

* The rules of succession. The crown was fixed in a single line, and the conditions on which a king's son succeeded his father were stated rather than left to the ''nawe''.
* The law. A body of enacted law, in place of the accumulation of custom, precedent and royal pronouncement by which the state had previously been governed.
* Legal institutions. Courts and officers with defined competence, to hear and determine the matters the law covered.
* Legislation. The manner in which law was to be made thereafter, which is to say the manner in which the enactments of the crown and the proceedings of the assemblies became law.

It was written in the Zong script. The instrument that fixed the Hachina constitution therefore also displaced the characters in which the Hachina had written for eight centuries, in the service of a state whose institutions were written down and legible to the power it dealt with. Whether the framework was composed in the Zong script or the traditional one and then translated is not established, and the question is the subject of dispute in the later literature.

Whether the framework was a single instrument or a body of enactments made over years is likewise unsettled. The record refers to it as one thing, and the surviving text implies compilation, but the compilations of the period include material that is later than the reign.

=== Hiknawe ===

The Hiknawe - ''hik'', "king", and ''nawe'', "council", "the king's assembly" - was created by the crown in this period on the model of the Zong assembly the embassy had observed.

Its function was to advise the king, and its members were drawn from the officials of the capital, the mercantile houses of the coast and the crown's own appointees, rather than from the great landholding houses and priestly lineages that made up the ''nawe''. It sat beside the ''nawe'' and competed with it, and to a king whose revenue came from foreign trade conducted by coastal houses, the Hiknawe was the more useful body.

The constitutional effect was to divide the government of the state between two assemblies with different memberships and different interests. The ''nawe'' elected the king and spoke for the land; the Hiknawe advised him and spoke for the trade.

The Hiknawe was also a concession, made in the same years as the change in the succession.

As the throne passed from father to son, the ''nawe'' lost the function that had given it its standing. A body that chooses the king is a body that cannot be ignored; a body that ratifies a birth is not. The great houses and the priestly lineages were left with the form of their old authority and none of its substance, and a court that had taken the substance had reason to offer something in its place.

What the crown offered was a second assembly, in which the coastal houses and the crown's own appointees sat and the elite could speak, on terms the crown set. The compensation was real but partial: the Hiknawe could be heard, but it could not choose, and its membership was not the membership of the ''nawe''. The election was not abolished outright; it was allowed to lapse while a substitute was provided.

The Hiknawe did not placate the traditionalists. Its members were the crown's appointees, the officials of the capital and the mercantile houses of the coast, and the lineages and priestly houses that had lost the choice of the king were not among them.

=== Traditionalist reaction ===

The script decree, the Zong manners of the court and the standing of the Hiknawe produced the first organised opposition in the recorded history of the state.

The movement drew on the priestly lineages and the temple schools, on the lineages of the plain whose standing the Hiknawe diminished, and on those for whom the displaced script was the record of the calendar and of the kings. Its argument was that the state was exchanging what made it central for what made it dependent - that a realm which wrote in another people's characters and dressed in their clothes had stopped being the centre of anything.

The movement is recorded in the period's own records, in the traditional script, which is itself evidence of what it was.

== Gold, and the turn to land ==

The revenue that had funded the empire's ascendancy failed in this period.

The gold came as tribute from [[Uwisa]] and [[Yelani]], and it was alluvial: the easy gold, the gold that could be taken from the streams and the surface ground, had been stripped over the preceding century. Deeper workings produced less at greater cost, and the tribute assessments, already raised, could not be raised far enough to make up the difference.

The consequence was a trade imbalance. The empire had spent a century buying Zong goods with gold, and now had less gold to buy with while the appetite for the goods remained. Imports continued; the means of paying for them did not.

=== Sale of the frontier lands ===

The court's response was to sell land.

The Hachina had themselves wanted plantations, having seen what they produced in Sinchew and having heard what they produced in [[Lacache]] before that. They lacked the knowledge and the machinery - the presses, the mills, the curing and the shipping - and could not establish the industry without instruction.

The frontier land was the former [[Kawesa]] country on the northern coast, which the empire had acquired in 1542 and had never settled with its own people. It was sold to Zong merchants, who wanted land in quantity and at a price below what they paid in the colony. The venders gained the price and, attached to the sales, the services of men who knew how to grow and process what would be grown on it.

The sales began a process of land privatisation along the northern coast that continued beyond the period. Land that had been the frontier of the empire became the property of foreign merchants, cultivated by their methods, and worked in the end by their labour.

== Horses ==

The supply of horses had depended since its beginning on the Zong trade. In this period the court attempted to end that dependence by breeding horses within the country.

The measure is recorded as an order of the crown for the establishment of studs on the plain, with animals obtained through the trade and pasture assigned from crown land. The object was strategic: a cavalry that could not be interrupted by a decision taken in a foreign port. How far the attempt succeeded is not established in the surviving records, and the horses of the later period cannot be distinguished by their origin in the documents that mention them.

== Eastern frontier ==

The empire's relations with the [[Yelani]] changed in this period from extraction to accommodation, and the reason was geographical.

=== Two Yelani ===

The Yelani hold two countries, not one. The Yelani of the mountains occupy the high belt that runs the length of the country's eastern side, all of it above 800 m, much of it above 1,500. The Yelani of the Aethiopean coast are a related people sharing the same speech, holding small chiefdoms in the wet and difficult country of the eastern seaboard.

They are treated separately in the record, and the distinction is real. Their history is shared, and in this period their politics began to converge, because the empire's interest gave them a common counterpart.

=== Problem of the mountains ===

The empire's goods reached the eastern seaboard only through the mountains, and the mountains are difficult, high and wet. There is no route across them that a trading party can take without the goodwill of the people who live in them.

This, rather than the gold, is why Hachinawa sought peace with the Yelani chiefdoms of the mountains. The empire could not reach the coast without them, and the coast was where the other powers were.

=== Illypnian traders ===

The Hachina learned in this period that the Zong are not the only foreign power, and that a second ocean lies on the far side of the mountains.

The [[Mercians|Mercian]] and [[Jacano|Jacanese]] traders had established small ports on the Aethiopean coast, on the country that would later be [[Etowah]]; and they had been in conflict with the Yelani peoples of that coast. The Hachina knew of them at first only by report, from traders on the eastern seaboard.

The court's interest was commercial and immediate. The empire had a diminished supply of gold and a demand for foreign goods. A second foreign power, buying on a second coast, was a new outlet - and in the judgement of the court, a better one than the Zong, against whom the terms of trade had been unfavourable for a century.

The approach to the coastal Yelani chiefdoms, made through the mountain chiefdoms as intermediaries, was therefore an attempt to reach the Illypnian market. The empire sought the goodwill of the two Yelani peoples because the mountains were in the way and the coast belonged to them.

=== Yelani ambition ===

The Yelani of the Aethiopean coast were not passive in this. They held small chiefdoms, they dealt with the Mercian and Jacanese traders directly, and they had ambitions of their own for the country behind their coast.

The convergence of the two Yelani peoples in this period is the beginning of a politics in which the empire, the mountain chiefdoms and the coastal chiefdoms each had something to gain from the others, and in which the foreign traders on the coast were the prize rather than the threat.

== End of the period ==

The period closes with the empire's revenue exhausted, its frontier land sold, its court divided between the Hiknawe and the ''nawe'', and its eastern policy turned to accommodation with a people it had been taxing for a century.

